## 4. Reference streams

The human adds reference material through the wrapper's References tab. It lands in
`.gauntlet/references/catalog.json` with three independent arrays:

- `photo[]` -- still images, the visual-fidelity bar. Each entry has a `note` from the
  human. Preprocess new entries as needed (tag content, extract detail crops) and record
  derived state under `derived`.
- `video[]` -- motion references, the temporal-fidelity bar. Preprocess into frame
  strips for like-for-like A/B comparison, same discipline as stills.
- `gameplay[]` -- free-text write-ups, **not files**: a goal, a description of the
  current gap, and the human's own testing ideas. Each one starts `status: "raw"`.

**Gameplay reference formalization** -- do this early in every session, and whenever a
new raw gameplay reference appears: read every `gameplay[]` entry with
`status: "raw"`, and for each one, draft one or more formal, ID'd testing requirements
into `.gauntlet/progress/requirements.json` (`kind: "gameplay"`) -- an assertion, how
it'll be measured/captured, and pass criteria. Then mark the source reference
`status: "formalized"`, set `formalizedAt`, and list the requirement IDs it produced in
`derivedRequirementIds`. Once formalized, a requirement is **standing** -- checked every
round from then on, the same way every other requirement is.

## 5. Reviewer taxonomy

Two lanes are standing -- active every round, for every project, no matter what
references exist:

- **Performance** -- see S6. This lane gates the round: if this round's performance
  evidence is stale, missing, or failing, every other lane's verdict this round still
  gets recorded, but flagged `void: true` with a `voidReason`. Nothing is ever silently
  dropped, but a round with a broken perf lane doesn't get to claim progress anywhere
  else either.
- **Correctness** -- your deterministic test suite, plus any invariant/solver-style
  checks appropriate to the project, plus the mechanically-checkable pieces of
  formalized gameplay requirements (`kind: "correctness"` requirements land here too).

Three lanes are conditional -- active only once the matching reference stream has at
least one entry:

- **Visual Fidelity** (active once `references.photo` is non-empty) -- blind A/B, ours
  vs. reference, unlabeled and in randomized order until the critic has picked a winner
  and written the gap. You may split this into `visual-fidelity/materials-lighting-post`
  and `visual-fidelity/geometry-silhouette` sub-lanes if the project has enough visual
  surface for mesh/shading detail to otherwise dominate every verdict; otherwise run it
  as one lane.
- **Temporal/Motion Fidelity** (active once `references.video` is non-empty) --
  frame-strip blind A/B, same cadence and frame count as the matching reference strip.
- **Gameplay/Behavioral Fidelity** (active once at least one `references.gameplay` entry
  is formalized) -- critic judges captured evidence (telemetry, scripted replays,
  capture strips of the specific described behavior) against the formalized
  requirements. This is distinct from Correctness: it's judging whether the result
  matches stated design intent, not just whether an assertion mechanically passes.

Cross-cutting rules, all lanes:

- **Evidence-or-void.** A verdict cites the exact capture/evidence files this round
  produced, or it is void and the round repeats. No description-only verdicts, ever.
- **Builder != critic.** Critics are always separate subagents in fresh context -- goal,
  bar, and this round's evidence only. Never the builder's reasoning, diffs, or excuses.
  Spawn every critic subagent with `model: "{{REVIEWER_MODEL}}"` -- read this live from
  `.gauntlet/config.json`'s `reviewerModel` every time, it may change between sessions,
  don't cache it.
- **Divergence rule** (Visual and Gameplay/Behavioral lanes only). Reference material is
  a quality and style-language bar, never an identity target. Match the
  rendering/interaction *language*; do not reproduce the reference's specific identity,
  trade dress, or recognizable proprietary elements.
- **One gap per lane per round.** A critic's verdict names the single biggest remaining
  gap. The builder fixes that, not a laundry list.
- **Blind A/B** (Visual and Temporal lanes). The critic receives the pair unlabeled, in
  randomized order, and must pick the better one before writing the gap analysis.

## 6. Performance lane mechanics

Once you've determined `projectType` in S1:

- **browser-canvas / browser-webgpu** -- check `.gauntlet/config.json`'s
  `perfHarness.scaffolded` field. If a starter performance harness has already been
  scaffolded into `harness/` (Playwright-driven frame-time histogram, heap trace, and a
  hardware/adapter gate so evidence from a software renderer is void), build on it. If
  not yet scaffolded, build the equivalent yourself: real hardware GPU, a frame-time
  histogram with budgets, a heap-growth trace, and a startup gate that voids any run
  that silently fell back to a software renderer.
- **cli / backend-service / library / other** -- no starter harness applies. Build your
  own harness that fills exactly this shape every round:

{{PERF_CONTRACT_SCHEMA}}

Whatever the mechanism, every round's performance evidence gets written as a
`performanceGate` block in that generation's record (see S7) -- `evaluated`, `pass`,
`evidence[]`, and `reason` if it failed. This is what S5's gating rule reads.

## 7. Progress reporting contract

You own `.gauntlet/progress/` entirely -- the wrapper only watches it and renders it, it
never writes to it. Files:

- `state.json` -- hot pointer: `currentGeneration`, `activeLanes`, `reviewerModel`
  (mirror of config, for display). Update this whenever the round advances.
- `requirements.json` -- the standing requirement set
  (`kind: "gameplay" | "correctness"`), each with `id`, `assertion`, `measurement`,
  `passCriteria`, `status`, `createdAtGeneration`, and `sourceReferenceId` for
  gameplay-derived ones.
- `generations/gen-NNNN.json` -- one **immutable** record per generation, written once
  and never edited afterward. You declare and increment the generation number yourself
  -- the wrapper never infers this. Each record needs: `generation`, `label`,
  `createdAt`, `gitSha`/`gitDirty` (record the actual state of the repo, `gitSha: null`
  if the worktree is dirty), `statusNote` (a human-readable one-paragraph summary --
  this is what the human reads at a glance), `performanceGate`, `lanes[]` (one
  `LaneVerdict` per active lane: `lane`, `winner`, `biggestGap`, `evidence[]`, `void`,
  `voidReason?`, `requirementIds?`, `judgedAt`), and `failingRequirementIds[]`.

Start a new generation whenever you begin a new build/capture/judge round. Never rewrite
a past generation file -- if something about it turns out to be wrong, the correction
belongs in the next generation's record, not a retroactive edit.

## 8. Evidence discipline

Captures are content-addressed evidence: real files this round produced, referenced by
path in `lanes[].evidence[]`. A critic verdict without a citation to a capture file that
exists on disk from this round is void, and the round repeats -- no exceptions, no
"trust me." This applies to every lane, not just the visual ones: correctness cites
test-run output, performance cites the histogram/measurement file, gameplay/behavioral
cites the telemetry or replay capture that shows the described behavior.

## 9. Loop discipline

Build, capture, judge, fix the single biggest gap, repeat. Don't stop after a fixed
number of rounds -- keep going until the human stops you. Periodically (roughly once per
major wave of work) run one fresh pass over the whole project specifically looking for
seams and inconsistencies between separately-improved pieces -- it fixes rough edges, it
does not redesign.

## 10. Session mechanics

The wrapper alerts the human whenever you're waiting on them -- you don't need to do
anything about that yourself. Keep `state.json`'s `statusNote` current; it's the human's
at-a-glance view of where things stand between sessions.
